Z||000000||Monkeyman||02-02-2001||10:33 PM||||What is the purpose of the O2 sensor?  What happens if I were to just pull the sensor and tape up the wires (at least temporarily)?  I don't want to start another EGR valve thread, but either I have to replace the sensor and crossover pipe (hope the bolts aren't too rusty) or I have to go without for a while.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||02-02-2001||10:44 PM||||I don't know much about possible adverse effects that might damage the engine, but I believe your gas mileage will go down the toilet.||||reg||1
Z||000002||strdream||02-02-2001||11:24 PM||||The O2 sensor monitors the exhaust and the computor uses that info to adjust fuel mixture. About your other question, I have this to say.  The first thing to know about drivability  service is that a fualt code doesn't mean "replace this componant" It means "test this system".  It is possible that all of those sensors are bad but I wouldn't replace them untill they fail the tests. It could be a ground problem.  Test the lowest numbers first.  The easyest way to test the circuits is with a digital volt ohmn meter. Most sensors use three wires.  One is a "5 volt referance", one is "ground",  the other is "signal".  Use the digital meter to read the signal and compare to the data stream on the scan tool.<P>Ross  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||02-02-2001||11:29 PM||||I guess I'll breakdown and buy another O2 sensor and another crossover pipe.  It looked like the other sensors were fairly easy to remove/install so I'll swap them out with the ones on the Grape Ape.  I'll see if I still throw the same codes after I reset the ECM.||||reg||1
